# dispatch — example crontab for unattended ticks (HANDOFF Pillar 1, issue #29)
# ===========================================================================
# This is the RECOMMENDED 1.0 prototype scheduler: a plain host crontab entry
# that runs the pipeline a few times a day under a lock. Edit the paths and the
# repo below, then install with:
#
#     crontab examples/dispatch.crontab
#
# DRY-RUN IS THE DEFAULT. The tick line below sets PIPELINE_DRY_RUN=0
# EXPLICITLY to go live — change it to PIPELINE_DRY_RUN=1 (or delete the
# assignment) to keep previewing without mutating GitHub. Never let a
# copy-paste accidentally run live: the explicit value is intentional.
#
# Overlapping ticks are serialized by the in-process flock the pipeline ships
# (E1-1, DISPATCH_LOCK_FILE) — a second tick that starts while one is running
# logs and exits 0 without double-claiming. The outer `flock -n` on the cron
# line is a belt-and-suspenders guard so a long-running tick never stacks.
#
# Logs are redirected to a file so each tick leaves a trace even though log()
# writes to stderr; the per-tick run record (E1-2, DISPATCH_RUN_RECORD) gives a
# structured start/end heartbeat alongside it.

# --- edit these to match your install -------------------------------------
DISPATCH_HOME=/opt/dispatch
SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin

# --- the tick: 4x/day at 00:07, 06:07, 12:07, 18:07 -----------------------
# Fields: min hour dom mon dow  command
# `cd $DISPATCH_HOME` so common.sh finds pipeline.env; the inline env vars are
# set explicitly (PIPELINE_REPO + the deliberate live flag); the outer flock
# guards against overlap; stdout+stderr append to the cron log.
7 0,6,12,18 * * * cd /opt/dispatch && PIPELINE_REPO=owner/your-repo PIPELINE_DRY_RUN=0 flock -n /tmp/dispatch.cron.lock ./entrypoint.sh --repo owner/your-repo >> /opt/dispatch/dispatch.cron.log 2>&1

# Upgrade paths (NOT the 1.0 default — see README "Scheduling unattended ticks"):
#   (a) OpenClaw Gateway — scheduler + webhook receiver + operator chat I/O.
#   (c) billy.maic VPS   — always-on remote host; see existing issue #15.
